Sanitized Linux 2.6.30 kernel headers (SDK copy), asm/unistd.h:
drop the __NR_accept4 definition (o32 4334).

The SDK header tree's syscall table runs to 334, but the hub
kernel's own rlx o32 table ends at 4331 (arch/rlx/kernel/
scall32-o32.S in the SDK source): the direct accept4 number
exists only in the header, not in the kernel. With the number
visible, uClibc-ng compiles its accept4 wrapper as a direct
INLINE_SYSCALL of 4334, which the hub can only answer ENOSYS -
a permanent unexplained post-2.6.30 invocation in the shipped
libc, tolerated by the ship gate only through a named exception
keyed to the NPTL build's symbol layout (the linuxthreads build
wears the same wrapper under its __libc_accept4 alias, where
the exception no longer matches).

With the definition removed from this build-time copy, the
wrapper takes its socketcall path instead (__NR_socketcall
4102, SYS_ACCEPT4): a number inside the hub kernel's table,
dispatched by its net/socket.c sys_socketcall, whose switch
carries a SYS_ACCEPT4 case. accept() is unaffected (MIPS has
its own __NR_accept, 4168) and no program in the image imports
accept4 either way. This is also shipped-build input 1's
original stated intent - that against the hub headers
"prlimit64/getrandom/statx/accept4 do not exist as wrappers" -
which held for the other three but was defeated for accept4
by this one header line.

Applied by build-shipped-runtime.sh to its build-time COPY of
the SDK header tree (build-gcc-uclibc-shipped/kernel-headers) -
the SDK tree under sources/ itself is never modified.

--- a/asm/unistd.h
+++ b/asm/unistd.h
@@ -352,7 +352,6 @@
 #define __NR_pwritev			(__NR_Linux + 331)
 #define __NR_rt_tgsigqueueinfo     (__NR_Linux + 332)
 #define __NR_perf_counter_open     (__NR_Linux + 333)
-#define __NR_accept4           (__NR_Linux + 334)
 
 /*
  * Offset of the last Linux o32 flavoured syscall
